---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import PasswordStrengthChecker from '../../components/PasswordStrengthChecker.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Password Strength Checker" description="Check a password against common strength criteria — length, character variety, and common password patterns.">
  <PasswordStrengthChecker client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why length matters more than complexity rules</h2>
  <p>Modern password guidance increasingly emphasizes length over forced complexity (special characters, mixed case) — a long passphrase is often both stronger and easier to remember than a short password crammed with mandatory symbol substitutions.</p>

  <h2>How to use this checker</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter a password to evaluate.</li>
    <li>Review its strength rating and any flagged weaknesses.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Password Strength Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using predictable substitutions</strong> (like "P@ssw0rd") that pattern-matching attacks specifically account for, providing little real security benefit over the base word.</li>
    <li><strong>Reusing a password across multiple accounts</strong> — even a strong password loses its protection once exposed in one breach if reused elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Relying on a strength meter alone</strong> without also checking the password against known-breached password lists, which a strength score doesn't capture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Password Strength Checker</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Checking a new password before setting it as an account credential</li>
    <li>Understanding what specifically makes a given password weak</li>
    <li>Teaching password hygiene concepts with concrete, visible feedback</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Password Strength FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this password sent anywhere for checking?</strong> No — the check runs entirely in your browser using local rules; nothing is transmitted or logged.</p>
</ToolLayout>
